score_margins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.1,"_gate_share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.07933126555097311,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.04855,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":32.4,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"HeartFlow, Inc., a medical technology company, provides non-invasive solutions for diagnosing and managing coronary artery diseases the United States and worldwide. Its HeartFlow Platform uses AI and computational fluid dynamics to creates a personalized 3D model of a patient's heart from a single coronary computed tomography angiography, a specialized type of scan that provides detailed images of the heart's arteries. The company provides Heartflow RoadMap Analysis that offers an intuitive anatomic visualization of the coronary arteries; Heartflow FFRCT Analysis, which calculates blood flow and pinpoints clinically; and Heartflow Plaque Analysis that provides a comprehensive assessment of coronary plaque, enabling optimized medical treatment strategies. Its platform provides insights on blood flow, stenosis, and plaque volume and composition by overcoming the limitations of traditional non-invasive imaging tests. HeartFlow, Inc. was formerly known as Cardiovascular Simulation, Inc. and changed its name to HeartFlow, Inc. in May 2009. The company was incorporated in 2007 and is headquartered in San Francisco, California.","ceo_bio":"Mr. John C.M. Farquhar | President, CEO & Director | Age 52 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 843 people. Revenue per employee of $209K ranks in the bottom quartile among Healthcare peers. Stock-based compensation of $17K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 7\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["Heartflow, Inc. is a mid-cap healthcare business with a $4.2B market cap on $176M of annual revenue.","Returns on capital run at -313% against a 10% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests. Gross margins of 77% confirm genuine pricing power.","The company is burning through $59M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, thin -4.2\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 4.9% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins 36% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 9.5% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $17.2M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Patent cliffs, drug-pricing reform, and reimbursement pressure \u2014 a single regulatory change can reset the pricing power that justifies the multiple.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Clinical trial concentration risk \u2014 binary FDA outcomes on a lead asset can wipe out billions in market cap in a single session.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 disruption across sub-sectors \u2014 weight-loss drugs are reshaping demand for orthopaedics, cardiovascular devices, and sleep-apnea treatments in ways not yet priced in.","sev":